# Storage: a local JSON document

There is no database and no server. The app is a static export; all data lives in
one JSON document held in the browser and synced to a private GitHub repo.

## Layers

- `src/lib/store/types.ts` — the `DbDoc` shape and its JSON (de)serialization.
- `src/lib/store/local-store.ts` — the in-memory document, IndexedDB persistence,
  `subscribe`/`mutate`, and id allocation.
- `src/lib/store/selectors.ts` — **pure** read functions of `(db, ...args)`.
- `src/lib/store/github-sync.ts` — pull/push against the GitHub Contents API.
- `src/db/queries/` — the async data-access API the rest of the app calls.

## Rules

- Never import `local-store` directly from a component or an action. Go through
  `src/db/queries/*` for writes, and `useStore(selector)` for reads.
- All read logic belongs in `selectors.ts` as a pure function so that pages and
  actions share one implementation.
- Mutations go through `mutate()`, which persists and schedules a sync push.
  Never assign to the document outside it.
- Timestamps are `Date` instances in memory and ISO strings in JSON. Anything
  added to `DbDoc` with a date must be handled in both `serializeDoc` and
  `deserializeDoc`, or it will arrive back as a string and silently misbehave.
- Relationships have no database to enforce them. Deleting a deck must explicitly
  remove its sub-decks and all their cards — see `collectDeckIdsToDelete`.
- Ids come from the `nextDeckId` / `nextCardId` counters. Never reuse an id, even
  for a deleted record, or sync merges become ambiguous.

## Keeping it small

Card HTML embeds images as base64, so the document is also the image store. It is
uploaded whole on every sync and GitHub refuses files over 100 MB. Downscale
images on the way in (`downscaleImage` in `rich-text-editor.tsx`) and be wary of
anything else that grows without bound.
